 "54535d89-12d6-55b4-922f-427d98b1be7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-325160" :question/problem "Find the **polar coordinates** of the point where the polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ intersects the negative $y$-axis. Express the polar angle in radians.\n\nThe point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{13}{2}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{13}{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/2399108905ce184b2b2d2d299f5e52539af01228b484316ece8b2efdd6a3b852.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{13}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-325160/field-1" "q-325160/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325168/field-1/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "9684ac91-4ec5-5270-a913-ca4c45328509" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325168/field-1/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "a135e380-f6f0-5022-b912-a7fae1b7f986" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325168/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "448d851e-ca56-513d-9fcf-44e43785aebf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325168/field-1/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer-field/choices ["q-325168/field-1/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" "q-325168/field-1/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325168" :question/id #uuid "ee108acb-13aa-5cba-b154-a8df1833a3bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-325168" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/2abff4f1dcb5bfd305a5560293892828f94d3fe8cca755bec1998d4f3fda077f.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(5,12)$, as shown above, is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=13.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325168/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325168"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-280257/field-1/0c8b08d4b198d89c5fe756f861bd5b37e398d2a5b3237908210d9646370a666f" :answer/id #uuid "378ee3c0-e556-543d-b5a7-d417409805ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-280257/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "57c1bb72-c884-5eef-b5fc-0768fb998a8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-280257/field-1/0c8b08d4b198d89c5fe756f861bd5b37e398d2a5b3237908210d9646370a666f" :answer-field/choices ["q-280257/field-1/0c8b08d4b198d89c5fe756f861bd5b37e398d2a5b3237908210d9646370a666f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-280257" :question/id #uuid "ebf95fa3-b7ba-500a-ab79-2c407acafc86" :question/math-academy-id "q-280257" :question/problem "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(2,4)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-280257/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-280257"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325177/field-1/7f76893339431fbe1025dfc34a81e64480edf5168d046d69b36669516297505c" :answer/id #uuid "7051fb3e-df22-557d-bea7-860bbd99904d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325177/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "f4b0ad18-c84b-5e54-a67c-6a2f293969d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325177/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b30cc0-d262-5f4d-8593-772f1da5138d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325177/field-1/7f76893339431fbe1025dfc34a81e64480edf5168d046d69b36669516297505c" :answer-field/choices ["q-325177/field-1/7f76893339431fbe1025dfc34a81e64480edf5168d046d69b36669516297505c" "q-325177/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325177" :question/id #uuid "b90809e4-4aff-5a7b-bad0-bfb4210baded" :question/math-academy-id "q-325177" :question/problem "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(6,2)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{10}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-325177/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection/c013b49be35595f2320dce494fd56397161e6766acfad1fc73db540e0f5b9cee" :answer/id #uuid "9ff354e2-c9c1-579e-b077-86e42cc3a8d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=225" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection/7502c3990a92a5af94dce0651918cf067358cc43c3f8cea54cfea9b12b6c6fe7" :answer/id #uuid "048bb022-0f69-56eb-a061-4d6d78fb6fef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=14\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection/029eb73d05410cf1b2988254ec5be89b7093db3bcbbd381dded3c7dedc076437" :answer/id #uuid "c319ffd0-f410-54d9-afe8-f6d8537e5adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection/841ea3d2664a10f89edc86375b267f18fb69e46d06fd8eabdc63949869d3e809" :answer/id #uuid "330a9fb9-7f0e-5341-9796-12e761088008" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=196" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection/0d8f7997a9ccd27f72c07aaf9529672b9506ba16d9f9f5e3d935463d5780c237" :answer/id #uuid "c56c197a-9947-5caa-8d0c-3ac3d3d84439" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=5\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dab361c-5b05-52d9-ad5a-150be3ef060b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120181/selection/029eb73d05410cf1b2988254ec5be89b7093db3bcbbd381dded3c7dedc076437" :answer-field/choices ["q-120181/selection/c013b49be35595f2320dce494fd56397161e6766acfad1fc73db540e0f5b9cee" "q-120181/selection/7502c3990a92a5af94dce0651918cf067358cc43c3f8cea54cfea9b12b6c6fe7" "q-120181/selection/029eb73d05410cf1b2988254ec5be89b7093db3bcbbd381dded3c7dedc076437" "q-120181/selection/841ea3d2664a10f89edc86375b267f18fb69e46d06fd8eabdc63949869d3e809" "q-120181/selection/0d8f7997a9ccd27f72c07aaf9529672b9506ba16d9f9f5e3d935463d5780c237"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120181" :question/id #uuid "8d8875f6-58ea-56c4-9273-75daba8efa6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120181" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/c3d18d92739af7c6ea7c2a8560ab54c4c08b8b20f9ee33ba52a254b16315d9bf.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(9,12)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=15.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120181/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120181"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5386"] :question/problem "What is the polar equation of the curve shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/2787b5cdecf8cda247811f40a661d2c72f6cac231d49f8157f8faca0d668da17.png)" :question/worked-solution "The given curve is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5387"] :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=2.7$ is a circle of radius $2.7$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $x$-axis at the point where $r=2.7$ and $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/04f57721c8672bf48aa6f07bf6c9571f826ab957d104a40b273254b4f50ff147.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $\\left(2.7, \\pi\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5388"] :question/problem "Find the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(2,5)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/assets/063df02b7846c476657a2deaa00937967707472a97b0e52a4a8b3dc5951ce1ab.png)" :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r = k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{29}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
